      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on some schema design and i'm currently at 2nf. can u walk me through the steps to get it to 3nf? i'm trying to figure out how to handle the transitive dependencies so we don't end up with a bunch of redundant info in the tables.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hey! Sure, I can help you with that. To move from 2NF to 3NF, you need to ensure that no non-prime attribute is transitively dependent on the primary key. If there are any transitive dependencies in your 2NF schema, you can identify them and modify your tables or create new tables to remove those dependencies.\n\nIn practical terms, you have to:\n\n1. Identify all the functional dependencies in your 2NF schema.\n2. Determine if any attributes are transitively dependent on the primary key; that is, they depend on another attribute that itself depends on the primary key.\n3. If you find any transitive dependencies, you'll need to eliminate them.\n\nTo do this, you can separate the tables with transitive dependencies by creating new tables. The new tables should have only the attributes that are involved in the transitive dependencies, and the original table should only contain attributes that are part of the primary key and are not transitive dependencies.\n\nFor example, if you have a table `Courses` with attributes `CourseID`, `InstructorName`, and `DepartmentID` and you notice that `InstructorName` is transitively dependent on `CourseID` (i.e., different courses have different instructors",
